# Project Best Practices

## Before Editing
- Clarify the task goal and constraints before making changes.
- Read nearby code and follow existing naming, structure, and patterns.
- Prefer small, focused changes over broad refactors unless requested.

## Implementation
- Keep code simple, explicit, and maintainable.
- Reuse existing utilities and abstractions before creating new ones.
- Handle errors intentionally; avoid silent failures and swallowed exceptions.
- Add concise comments only for non-obvious logic.

## Validation
- Run targeted checks for changed areas (tests, typecheck, lint) when available.
- Fix issues introduced by your change before finishing.
- If full validation is too expensive, clearly report what was run and what was not.

## Safety and Git Hygiene
- Do not commit secrets, credentials, or generated artifacts unless explicitly requested.
- Avoid destructive git commands (force push, hard reset, checkout discard) unless explicitly requested.
- Never revert unrelated user changes; limit edits to task-relevant files.

## Communication
- Summarize what changed, why it changed, and any trade-offs.
- Include concrete next steps when validation could not be completed.
